JPEG-in-JXL bit-shaving session — lever audit (2026-05-28)
============================================================

Parent commit: 8bd46f89 main (session-final at +1.522% vs cjxl on 200-file).

Goal: Sweep 4 small levers from the round-2 research catalogue
(A: kJpegTranscodeACMeta tree, B: quant-matrix RAW mode use_global_tree,
C: frame all_default for JPEG, D: DC/QF threshold value encoding selectors)
to capture cumulative bit savings beyond the +1.52pp ceiling reached by
the prior session.

Bench corpus: 200 JPEGs (66 baseline + 134 progressive) drawn from
`/home/lilith/product-images` + `/home/lilith/work/codec-corpus` via
`random.seed(11)`. Stratified to match the ~33/67 baseline/progressive
split observed in the corpus. SAME corpus used by
`jpeg_in_jxl_recompression_2026-05-28.tsv` (session-start measurement).

Encoders:
- cjxl: libjxl v0.12.0 4279d48 (`/home/lilith/work/jxl-efforts/libjxl/build/tools/cjxl`)
- ours: `cjxl-rs` (this repo, `--no-default-features --features
  'jpeg-reencoding,parallel,jxl_encoder/std'`)

Roundtrip verification: djxl v0.12.0 d2c7032 `--reconstruct_jpeg` via
extension auto-detect, byte-identical compare against source.

Per-lever audit
---------------

LEVER A — kJpegTranscodeACMeta tree (and matching token collectors)
    Status: RULED OUT, opt-in API shipped (default OFF).
    Implementation: new `JPEG_TRANSCODE_CONTEXT_TREE_TOKENS` static
    (243 vs 313 tokens; AC-meta subtree shrinks 11 leaves → 1
    `Leaf(Zero)`, DC subtree unchanged 34-leaf gradient-fixed BSP);
    new `write_jpeg_transcode_context_tree`; new
    `collect_dc_tokens_region_jpeg_transcode` (DC contexts shift
    11..=44 → 1..=34); new
    `collect_ac_metadata_tokens_region_jpeg_transcode` (all tokens
    at ctx 0 with raw values, no gradient/left/zero residuals).
    Dispatch gated on `jpeg_transcode_tree_enabled()` which reads
    `JPEG_LEVER_A_ENABLE=1`; default OFF after measurement.

    Per-200-file paired bench (delta vs cjxl):
      | mode             | total bytes | delta vs cjxl |
      |------------------|-------------|---------------|
      | baseline (off)   | 45,484,995  | +1.5225%      |
      | lever A on       | 49,326,710  | +10.0972%     |
      | delta            | +3,841,715  | +8.5747pp     |

    Per-file impact (single example, 81xLs_0j9dL.jpg):
      - dc_global       853B → 824B (-29B; tree shrink ✓)
      - dc_groups       29,444B → 44,828B (+15,384B; CFL data regression ✗)
      - net             243,674B → 259,029B (+15,355B; +6.3% per file)

    Root cause: collapsing 11 AC-metadata contexts to 1 context with
    `Predictor::Zero` removes the gradient/left predictor residuals
    that kept tokens close to 0. Even though typical JPEGs produce
    near-zero CFL multipliers (per-tile YtoX/YtoB → all zeros on the
    3 sampled images), the EPF channel emits raw sharpness values
    (e.g. 4) and the merged-context Huffman code can't be as tight
    per-context as the multi-context baseline. Even when most tokens
    are 0, the merged distribution forces longer codes for the
    minority symbols.

    libjxl's own JPEG-transcode path pays the same cost — we are
    *already* better than libjxl on the AC-metadata stream by using
    a richer tree (matches libjxl's `kACMeta` shape, NOT
    `kJpegTranscodeACMeta`).

    Disposition: keep the API surface so future investigators can
    A/B measure other AC-metadata routing strategies without
    re-implementing the ~200 LOC token collectors. Production
    default remains OFF.

LEVER B — quant matrix RAW mode encoding
    Status: AT PARITY, no change shipped.
    Investigation: read both libjxl `enc_quant_weights.cc::EncodeQuant`
    and our `write_raw_quant_table_modular`. Both use
    `ModularGenericCompress`-style sub-bitstream:
      GroupHeader → MA tree (Decoder::parse 6 ctx) → tree tokens →
      Data entropy (Decoder::parse 1 ctx) → 192 data tokens.
    Our per-file qtable overhead ~350-400 bits (~50 B). libjxl
    structurally produces the same bytes via the same code path.

    libjxl's only structural difference: the quant table stream
    participates in the GLOBAL tree (`tree_splits_.push_back(qt0
    ID)` in `enc_modular.cc:663`), so the qtable doesn't write its
    own tree+entropy header. Estimated savings if we moved to
    use_global_tree=true: ~80 bits/file (~10 B) = ~2,000 B on 200
    files = ~0.005pp. Not worth the multi-day refactor.

LEVER C — frame all_default for JPEG
    Status: STRUCTURALLY IMPOSSIBLE for YCbCr JPEGs.
    Investigation: traced libjxl `frame_header.cc::VisitFields`.
    When `xyb_encoded=false` (JPEG mode), the field visitor reads
    `Bool(false, &alternate)` where `alternate=(color_transform ==
    kYCbCr)`. For YCbCr JPEGs this MUST be `1` (true), which fails
    the AllDefault visitor check (default value 0). Therefore
    `is_all_default()` can NEVER return true for our JPEG output
    (every 3-component baseline/progressive JPEG is encoded with
    `do_ycbcr=true`).

    Our existing `FrameHeader::is_all_default()` correctly
    enforces `xyb_encoded=true` as a precondition. Lever C cannot
    save bytes on the JPEG transcode path. Skipped without code
    change.

LEVER D — DC/QF threshold value encoding selectors
    Status: AT PARITY, no change shipped.
    Investigation: read libjxl `entropy_coder.h:37-42`:
      kDCThresholdDist = U32Enc(Bits(4), BitsOffset(8,16),
                                BitsOffset(16,272),
                                BitsOffset(32,65808))
      kQFThresholdDist = U32Enc(Bits(2), BitsOffset(3,4),
                                BitsOffset(5,12),
                                BitsOffset(8,44))
    Our `write_dc_threshold` / `write_qf_threshold` in
    `vardct/context_tree.rs:632-690` use IDENTICAL selectors and
    payload widths. Already at parity.

Cumulative result
-----------------

| metric                      | baseline       | with lever A   | delta  |
|-----------------------------|----------------|----------------|--------|
| roundtrip OK                | 200 / 200      | 200 / 200      | 0      |
| roundtrip DIFF              | 0 / 200        | 0 / 200        | 0      |
| total source bytes          | 54,738,069     | 54,738,069     | 0      |
| total cjxl bytes            | 44,802,893     | 44,802,893     | 0      |
| total ours bytes            | 45,484,995     | 49,326,710     | +8.45% |
| delta vs cjxl (pp)          | +1.5225%       | +10.0972%      | +8.57pp|

Net session deliverable: 0pp byte gain.
- 3 levers (B/C/D) confirmed AT PARITY or STRUCTURALLY IMPOSSIBLE
  via direct libjxl source read; no production code touched for
  those.
- 1 lever (A) implemented end-to-end (~200 LOC tree + collectors
  + dispatch), measured at 200-file scale, RULED OUT as default
  per +8.57pp regression. Opt-in API surface retained for future
  investigators.

What was NOT closed (the +1.52pp remaining gap)
-----------------------------------------------

Section breakdown of 81xLs_0j9dL.jpg (one of the bench files,
+1.49% over cjxl = +3,584 B):
  - file_hdr       8 B
  - frame_hdr      <1 B
  - dc_global      853 B
  - dc_groups      29,444 B
  - ac_global      4,834 B
  - ac_groups      207,814 B
  Total           242,953 B (= 243,674 - 720 container/JBRD)

cjxl total: 240,090 B = same container layout (jbrd 483 B
identical, brob ~66 B). The 3,584 B delta is entirely in the JXL
codestream, distributed across all sections proportionally.

Confirmed at parity with cjxl:
- HybridUint config (kNone for JPEG path, lever #3 in prior session)
- Histogram clustering (kFast for JPEG, no enhanced pair-merge)
- LZ77 (disabled, matches libjxl JPEG behaviour)
- ANS histogram strategy (we use Precise = MORE search than cjxl's
  Approximate; should be at-or-better)
- Context map encoding (3-way simple/Huffman+MTF/ANS+LZ77 selector)
- block_ctx_map (JPEG DC-quantile from issue #65)
- Custom coefficient orders (EX-J17a Lehmer cost-gated)
- DC/QF threshold value encoding (lever D verified at parity)
- jbrd payload (parity verified: 483 B vs 483 B)

Remaining 1.52pp is in accumulated tiny libjxl tunings, each
worth ~0.05pp or smaller per round-2 research. The structural
levers (A/B/C/D) have all been measured and either ship or are
ruled out.

Probes / instrumentation added (env-var-gated, zero default cost)
-----------------------------------------------------------------

- `JPEG_SECTION_DUMP=1` — print per-section bytes (file_hdr,
  frame_hdr, dc_global, dc_groups, ac_global, ac_groups) and
  token counts (dc_tokens, ac_meta_tokens, ac_tokens).
- `JPEG_LEVER_A_ENABLE=1` — opt-in to the lever A
  (kJpegTranscodeACMeta tree + matching token collectors); see
  `jpeg_transcode_tree_enabled()` in `jpeg/encode.rs`.

Repro
-----

1. Build: `cargo build --release -p jxl-encoder-cli
            --no-default-features
            --features 'jpeg-reencoding,parallel,jxl_encoder/std'`
2. Bench script: `/tmp/bit-shaving/bench_lever.py` (driver),
   `/tmp/bit-shaving/bench_quick.py` (baseline). Both walk the
   first N files from
   `benchmarks/jpeg_in_jxl_recompression_2026-05-28.tsv`,
   encode via `cjxl-rs`, decode via `djxl --reconstruct_jpeg`,
   compare bytes. Pass extra env via `KEY=VAL` positional args
   (e.g. `JPEG_LEVER_A_ENABLE=1`).
3. Compare deltas across modes.

